(PAGE 5: SCIENTIFIC CROP SELECTION FRAMEWORK) '
(The 6-Pillar Decision Framework for Crop & Variety Selection:) '
(Pillar 1: Soil Characteristics:) '
(- Heavy Clay Soils \\(Vertisols\\): High water retention; ideal for Cotton, Soybean, Wheat) '
(- Sandy Loam Soils \\(Inceptisols/Entisols\\): Excellent aeration; ideal for Potato, Groun) '
(- Saline/Alkaline Soils: Choose tolerant crops \\(Barley, Mustard, Cotton, Beetroot\\).) '
(Pillar 2: Water Availability & Irrigation Source:) '
(- Tubewell with canal support: Water-intensive crops \\(Paddy, Sugarcane, Banana, Vegetabl) '
(- Limited Tubewell / Deep Water Table: Micro-irrigated crops \\(Mustard, Chickpea, Drip Ma) '
(- Purely Rainfed \\(Dryland\\): Pearl millet, Moth bean, Cluster bean, Castor, Niger, Sorg) '
(Pillar 3: Agro-Climatic Temperature Windows:) '
(- Verify chilling hours for temperate crops and verify terminal heat tolerance for rabi ce) '
(Pillar 4: Market Connectivity & Cold Chain:) '
(- Perishables \\(Tomato, Strawberry, Capsicum\\) require proximity \\(<4 hours\\) to mandi) '
(Pillar 5: Labor Availability vs Mechanization:) '
(- Labor-scarce areas must prioritize machine-harvestable crops \\(Combine-harvested Wheat/) '
(Pillar 6: Crop Rotation & Soil Health Index:) '
(- Avoid planting the same botanical family consecutively \\(e.g. Tomato after Potato or Ch) '
ET
endstream
